<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golconda Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Golconda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Golconda Fort is a fortress on a granite hill west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. First built as a mud fort under the Kakatiyas, it passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musunuri-nayakas">Musunuri Nayaks</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> in the 1360s and became the capital of the Qutb Shahi sultans, who rebuilt it in stone. It gave its name to the diamonds traded there and fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in 1687 after an eight-month siege.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golconda-fort.jpg" alt="Golconda Fort" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golconda_Fort_005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
